## 1. Why Java Enterprise Edition (J2EE)?

Java SE (core Java) alone is not enough to build applications that many users can access remotely over the internet. **J2EE (Java Enterprise Edition)** gives us the concept and tools to build **web applications** — applications that can be accessed remotely by any user who has:

- An active **internet connection**, and
- A **browser**

> **Interview one-liner:** J2EE extends core Java with APIs (Servlets, JSP, etc.) needed to build multi-tier, network-based (web) applications.
> 

---

## 2. What is a Web Application?

A **web application** is an application that can be **remotely accessed** by a user via a browser, over a network, using HTTP.

To host and run a web application, you need a **server**.

---

## 3. What is a Server?

A **server** is a system/computer with powerful hardware **and** server software.

- **Server software** handles the **request** and **response** objects — i.e., it listens for incoming client requests and sends back responses.
- Any normal computer can be converted into a "server system" by installing server software on it.

### Popular Server Software

| Server Software | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| Apache Tomcat | Most compatible/popular for Java (Servlet/JSP) applications |
| SAP Server | Used with SAP-based enterprise systems |
| JBoss (WildFly) | Full Java EE application server |
| Nginx | Popular for static content / reverse proxy |

> For Java programs specifically, **Apache Tomcat** is the most commonly used and compatible server software.
> 

