## 4. Tomcat & Web Container

When you download and set up **Tomcat**, it provides an **environment** called the **Web Container**.

- The Web Container is also known as **Catalina**.
- Inside this container, you create and deploy your **projects** (web applications).

```
Tomcat Server
   └── Web Container (a.k.a Catalina)
         └── Your Project (e.g., "J")
               ├── index.html
               ├── other .html files
               └── Servlets (Java classes)
```

**Logic/Analogy:** Think of Tomcat as an apartment building (server), and the Web Container (Catalina) as the management system that allows multiple "flats" (your web projects/apps) to be hosted and run inside it.

---

## 5. Project Structure of a Dynamic Web Project

When you create a project in an IDE (like Eclipse) as a **Dynamic Web Project**, the folder structure looks like this:

```
ProjectName (e.g., "J")
└── src (Java Resources)
      └── main
            ├── java              → Your Java packages & classes (Servlets go here)
            └── webapp             → Your HTML/JSP/CSS/JS files go here
                    ├── index.html
                    ├── output.html
                    └── WEB-INF
                          └── web.xml
```

### Key Rules

- **HTML files** must be created inside `src/main/webapp`.
- **Java classes (Servlets)** are created inside `src/main/java` under a package (e.g., `com.tap`).
- By default, the container looks for **`index.html`** as the default/welcome file when the project is executed.

