## 11. Why HTML Can't Call a Servlet Directly — web.xml

### The Problem

If you set the HTML form's `action` directly to the Servlet name (e.g., `action="MyServlet"`), Tomcat throws a **404 Not Found** error. The front-end HTML **cannot directly communicate** with a Servlet class.

### The Analogy (from the lecture)

> Think of it like asking your father (Servlet) for trip money directly — it doesn't work. You need an intermediary — your **mother** — who negotiates with your father on your behalf.
> 

In web applications, this "intermediary" role is played by **`web.xml`** (the Deployment Descriptor).

```
index.html (You)
     ↓  (form submission — asks for something)
web.xml (Mom — the middle-person / mapping file)
     ↓  (maps URL pattern → correct Servlet)
Servlet (Dad — the one who actually processes and responds)
```

### Why is this needed?

- The browser only understands **URLs**, not Java class names.
- `web.xml` acts as a **mapping/configuration layer** that tells the container: *"When a request comes in for URL pattern X, forward it to Servlet class Y."*

> **Interview one-liner:** `web.xml` is the **Deployment Descriptor** that decouples the client-facing URL from the actual server-side Java class, enabling flexible URL mapping.
> 

---

## 12. web.xml Configuration in Detail

`web.xml` resides inside `WEB-INF` folder and uses **two key tags**:

### 12.1 `<servlet>` Tag — declares the Servlet

```xml
<servlet>
    <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
    <servlet-class>com.tap.MyServlet</servlet-class>
</servlet>
```

- `<servlet-name>` → a logical/alias name you choose for the Servlet
- `<servlet-class>` → the **fully qualified class name** (package + class name)

### 12.2 `<servlet-mapping>` Tag — maps a URL pattern to the Servlet

```xml
<servlet-mapping>
    <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
    <url-pattern>/callServlet</url-pattern>
</servlet-mapping>
```

- `<servlet-name>` → **must exactly match** the name used in `<servlet>`
- `<url-pattern>` → the URL path the client will call. **Must start with a `/`** (this is a very common error — see next section)

### Full web.xml Example

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<web-app xmlns="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee"
         version="4.0">

    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>com.tap.MyServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>

    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/callServlet</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>

</web-app>
```

### And in `index.html`:

```html
<form action="callServlet" method="get">
    Name: <input type="text" name="name">
    <input type="submit">
</form>
```

### The Complete Flow

```
1. User fills form in index.html and clicks Submit
2. Browser sends request to "callServlet" (the url-pattern)
3. Tomcat checks web.xml → finds <url-pattern>/callServlet</url-pattern>
4. web.xml tells it → servlet-name = CallServlet
5. Tomcat finds <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name> → gets <servlet-class>com.tap.MyServlet</servlet-class>
6. Tomcat loads MyServlet class and calls its service() method
7. service() reads request data and writes response data
```

