## 14. Request and Response Objects

When a client submits a form, the container automatically creates **two objects**:

| Object | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `HttpServletRequest` (`request`) | Carries data **sent from client to server** (form field values, headers, etc.) |
| `HttpServletResponse` (`response`) | Used to **send data back from server to client** (HTML output, headers, status codes) |

### 14.1 Reading Data — `request.getParameter()`

To read a value submitted from an HTML form field, use:

```java
String userName = request.getParameter("name");
```

- The **string passed** to `getParameter()` **must exactly match** the `name` attribute of the HTML input field.

```html
<input type="text" name="name">
```

```java
String userName = request.getParameter("name"); // matches "name" attribute above
```

### 14.2 Writing Output — `response.getWriter()`

To send output/HTML back to the browser:

```java
PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
out.print("Welcome " + userName);
```

- `response.getWriter()` returns a **`PrintWriter`** object.
- Use `out.print()` / `out.println()` to write content that will be rendered in the **browser** (unlike `System.out.println()`, which prints to the **server console**, not the browser).

| Method | Prints To |
| --- | --- |
| `System.out.println()` | Server console (IDE/terminal logs) |
| `response.getWriter().print()` | Client browser (visible to the user) |

### 14.3 Sending HTML Tags from a Servlet

You can even send HTML markup as part of the Servlet's output string:

```java
out.print("<h1>Welcome " + userName + "</h1>");
```

**Why does this work?**

> For Java, this is just a `String` value. But when the string reaches the **browser**, the browser interprets `<h1>` as an actual HTML tag and renders it accordingly (bold, large heading, etc.).
> 

