## 15. Full End-to-End Example

### `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
    <form action="callServlet" method="get">
        Name: <input type="text" name="name"><br>
        <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

### `web.xml` (inside `WEB-INF`)

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<web-app xmlns="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee" version="4.0">

    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>com.tap.MyServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>

    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>CallServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/callServlet</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>

</web-app>
```

### `MyServlet.java` (inside `com.tap` package)

```java
package com.tap;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

public class MyServlet extends HttpServlet {

    protected void service(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Step 1: Read the value from the request object
        String userName = request.getParameter("name");

        // Step 2: Get a PrintWriter to send output back to the browser
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();

        // Step 3: Print dynamic, personalized output
        out.print("<h1>Welcome " + userName + "</h1>");
    }
}
```

### Result

- User types "Alex" → clicks Submit → browser shows **"Welcome Alex"**
- User types "Rohit" → clicks Submit → browser shows **"Welcome Rohit"**

✅ This is now **truly dynamic output**, unlike the earlier static `output.html` approach.

